---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import PdfSecureCompressToSize from '@/components/tools/pdf/PdfSecureCompressToSize.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="compress-pdf-to-300kb">
  <PdfSecureCompressToSize client:load targetBytes={307200} />

  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <section>
      <h2>The comfortable middle</h2>
      <p>
        At 300 KB, a multi-page document no longer looks compressed. It is about 50 KB a page across
        six pages: enough for a scan at about 110 DPI at a JPEG quality without visible blocking. At
        that point most people cannot see that the file changed.
      </p>
      <p>
        You see it as a limit on contract-submission portals, some medical-records upload forms and
        grant applications. People also choose it when they are told an attachment is "too big" with
        no number.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Resolution, in plain terms</h2>
      <p>
        DPI is the number of pixels rendered for each inch of the printed page. An A4 page is 8.27 inches
        wide, so:
      </p>
      <div class="overflow-x-auto">
        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th scope="col">DPI</th>
              <th scope="col">A4 page in pixels</th>
              <th scope="col">Looks like</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td class="tabular-nums">72</td>
              <td class="tabular-nums">595 × 842</td>
              <td>Fine at 100% zoom; small print softens close up</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="tabular-nums">96</td>
              <td class="tabular-nums">794 × 1123</td>
              <td>The old Windows screen standard: readable, ordinary</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="tabular-nums">110</td>
              <td class="tabular-nums">910 × 1287</td>
              <td>The usual sweet spot: sharp on a laptop and on a phone</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="tabular-nums">150</td>
              <td class="tabular-nums">1240 × 1754</td>
              <td>Prints acceptably; footnotes and stamps stay sharp</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="tabular-nums">200</td>
              <td class="tabular-nums">1654 × 2339</td>
              <td>Near print quality; only worth it with a large byte budget</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p>
        Two times the DPI gives four times the pixels, so DPI is the most expensive setting on the page.
        At a 300 KB target, the automatic setting usually chooses 96 to 150 DPI, depending on the number
        of pages.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Colour or grayscale?</h2>
      <p>
        A JPEG stores brightness at full resolution and colour at half in each direction. The two chroma
        planes together are about a sixth to a third of the bytes of a typical scan, and more when the
        scan has coloured noise, which camera-based scans always have.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          Turn grayscale on for black-ink documents, photocopies, printed letters, contracts and
          documents you will print in mono.
        </li>
        <li>
          Leave it off when colour carries meaning: a stamped certificate, a highlighted passage, a
          chart, an identity document, or a signature where blue ink must look different from black.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Why "300 KB" rarely comes out at exactly 300 KB</h2>
      <p>
        The search stops at the largest quality that still fits. JPEG output size moves in steps, so the
        last accepted attempt is usually a little under the target: 291 KB, 297 KB, 283 KB. This is
        correct, because the alternative is a file over the limit that the form rejects.
      </p>
      <p>
        The result panel shows both numbers with a bar to compare them, and the arithmetic of the
        search: your target, minus the estimated container overhead, divided by the page count.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Compressing on a phone</h2>
      <p>
        The tool works on mobile, which matters because people often need to get a file under 300 KB
        on a phone, close to a deadline. Two notes:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          Mobile browsers limit the memory of a tab. A very large scan, such as 40 pages at 600 DPI,
          can use it up, and the tab reloads. Split the document first, or use a laptop.
        </li>
        <li>
          Rendering uses the CPU, so expect two to four times the time of a laptop. Progress shows per
          page.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </section>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
